# Hydrazoic_acid was added on 10/1/2026. Like Internal_phosphate_polyphos_chain
# and Initial_phosphate_like_in_ATP_ADP, it was not determined from the
# training data. The neutralizer adds a proton to the terminal N- of HN3,
# giving H-N=[N+]=N-H, which Azide also matches. Azide removes only one proton
# and claims the other nitrogen as context, so the azide anion was never
# produced. This entry claims both protons first. The first (match index 1)
# exists in water only as an artifact of neutralization, so it uses the same
# always-deprotonated convention as Nitro. The second (match index 3) is the
# HN3 N-H, for which the Azide mean and standard deviation are reused; they
# match the aqueous value:
#   hydrazoic acid 4.65 (25 C, I=0): quoted in Betterton EA, Robinson JL
#     (1997) J Air Waste Manage Assoc, doi:10.1080/10473289.1997.10464060
#     (compiled value; primary source not yet verified)
*Hydrazoic_acid	[H]-[N+0:1]=[N+:2]=[N+0:3]-[H]	1	-1000.0	0	3	4.65	0.07071067811865513

*Azide	[N+0:1]=[N+:2]=[N+0:3]-[H]	2	4.65	0.07071067811865513
# N_nitro_amide was added on 10/1/2026 and was not determined from the
# training data. An N-H flanked by a nitro group is a strong acid, but no
# entry reached it: Nitro matched first and, because it claimed the atom its
# nitro group hangs from, protected the very nitrogen in question, so
# Amide_electronegative was rejected and the neutral molecule was returned.
# This entry claims only the nitrogen and its hydrogen, naming the nitro group
# and the carbonyl in recursive constraints rather than matching them, and
# Nitro below does the same for its substituent. The two matches are then
# disjoint, so both sites fire and the nitro group is still deprotonated. The
# first clause accepts the nitro group as the neutralizer leaves it or in its
# charge-separated form, in case neutralization is skipped. Requiring a
# carbonyl as well as the nitro keeps this to the amides, ureas, and
# carbamates the two measurements come from; plain nitramines are a separate
# class and keep whatever entry they reach now. The mean and standard
# deviation come from:
#   N-nitrourethane 3.28 and nitrourea 4.57: Bell RP, Higginson WCE (1949)
#     Proc R Soc A 197:141, as compiled by R. Williams (ACS Division of
#     Organic Chemistry, updated 4/7/2022)
*N_nitro_amide	[NX3+0;H1;$([NX3]-[NX3](=[OX1])-[OX2]),$([NX3]-[N+](=[OX1])-[OX1-]);$([NX3]-[CX3]=[OX1]):1]-[H]	0	3.925	0.9121677477306467

Nitro	[NX3;$([NX3]-[#6,#7,#8]):1](=[O:2])-[O:3]-[H]	2	-1000.0	0
# Nitroguanidine was added on 10/1/2026 and was not determined from the
# training data. AmidineGuanidine1 matches any carbon bearing three nitrogens
# and assigns it 12.03, but a nitro group on one of those nitrogens removes
# the basicity almost entirely: nitroguanidine forms salts only with strong
# acids. The entry below therefore returned nitroarginine and its relatives as
# cations. Before the Nitro entry stopped claiming the atom its nitro group
# hangs from, these molecules had no guanidine site at all and came out
# neutral, which was the right answer for the wrong reason; this entry makes
# it the right answer for the right one. The nitro group is named in a
# recursive constraint rather than matched, so that Nitro can still claim it
# and both sites fire on the one molecule. Nitroamidines, which carry two
# nitrogens rather than three, are not covered here and keep the generic rule.
# The mean is the measured basic constant:
#   nitroguanidine -0.98 (25 C, a1): Taylor PJ, Wait AR (1986) J Chem Soc
#     Perkin Trans II, 1765-1770
# A single value has no spread, so the standard deviation of 1.0 follows
# Internal_phosphate_polyphos_chain and Carboxamide. Unlike the -1000
# convention used by Nitro, this leaves the site able to protonate if someone
# asks for a strongly acidic pH, which is what the measurement describes.
Nitroguanidine	[N:1]-[C;$(C-[NX3]-[NX3](=[OX1])-[OX2]),$(C-[NX3]-[N+](=[OX1])-[OX1-]):2](-[N:3])=[NX2:4]-[H:5]	3	-0.98	1.0

# Nitroimine_guanidine was added on 10/1/2026 and was not determined from the
# training data. It is the other place a nitro group can sit on a guanidine:
# on the imine nitrogen rather than on an amino nitrogen, which is the form
# the neonicotinoid insecticides take. Nitroguanidine above requires a
# hydrogen there and so does not reach them, and they fell through to
# AmidineGuanidine2, whose window starts at 7.90, producing a cation at
# physiological pH. As with that entry, the nitro group is named in a
# recursive constraint rather than matched, so Nitro can still claim it.
# Thiamethoxam is the compound that surfaced this; imidacloprid, clothianidin,
# nitenpyram, and dinotefuran share the motif. The mean is the measured basic
# constant of the class archetype:
#   imidacloprid 1.56 (25 C, a1): Chamberlain K, Evans AA, Bromilow RH (1996)
#     Pestic Sci 47:265-272
# The same source reports 11.12 as its a2, which is the N-H acidity of the
# imidazolidine rather than a basicity and is not used here. A single value
# has no spread, so the standard deviation of 1.0 follows
# Internal_phosphate_polyphos_chain and Carboxamide.
Nitroimine_guanidine	[N:1]-[C:2](-[N:3])=[NX2;$(N-[NX3](=[OX1])-[OX2]),$(N-[N+](=[OX1])-[OX1-]):4]	3	1.56	1.0

AmidineGuanidine1	[N:1]-[C:2](-[N:3])=[NX2:4]-[H:5]	3	12.025333333333334	1.5941046150769165

# Imine nitrogens bearing a cyano, sulfonyl, or oxygen substituent are
# excluded (e.g., N-cyanoguanidines, N-sulfonylguanidines, amidoximes). These
# substituents remove most of the basicity this group was fit to.
# Amidine_carbon_substituted was added on 10/1/2026 and was not determined
# from the training data. AmidineGuanidine1 requires three nitrogens on the
# central carbon, so it reaches guanidines but not amidines; amidines fell
# through to AmidineGuanidine2, whose window starts at 7.90 and so produced a
# neutral amidine at physiological pH. This entry covers amidines whose carbon
# bears one amino nitrogen, one imine nitrogen, and a carbon or hydrogen, with
# both nitrogens carrying only carbon or hydrogen. N-aryl, N-acyl,
# N-sulfonyl, N-cyano, and amidoxime variants are excluded, since conjugation
# or an electronegative substituent removes several units of the basicity this
# entry is fit to, and they keep the generic rule. Guanidines are excluded by
# requiring carbon or hydrogen as the third substituent, so they still reach
# AmidineGuanidine1. The mean and standard deviation come from the two aqueous
# amidine pKaH values in the pKa compilation of R. Williams (ACS Division of
# Organic Chemistry, updated 4/7/2022,
# organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/pka-compilation-williams.pdf),
# both attributed there to:
#   acetamidine 12.52 and benzamidine 11.6: Albert A, Goldacre R, Phillips J
#     (1948) J Chem Soc 2240
Amidine_carbon_substituted	[#6,#1:1]-[CX3:2](-[NX3+0;!$(N-[!#6;!#1]);!$(N-c);!$(N-C=[O,S]);!$(N-C#N);!$(N-S(=O)=O):3])=[NX2+0;!$(N-[!#6;!#1]);!$(N-c);!$(N-C=[O,S,N]);!$(N-C#N);!$(N-S(=O)=O):4]	3	12.059999999999999	0.6505382386916236

AmidineGuanidine2	[C:1](-[N:2])=[NX2+0;!$(N-C#N);!$(N-S(=O)=O);!$(N-[OX2]):3]	2	10.035538461538462	2.1312826469414716

Sulfate	[SX4:1](=[O:2])(=[O:3])([O:4]-[C,c,N,n:5])-[OX2:6]-[H]	5	-2.36	1.3048043093561141
Sulfonate	[SX4:1](=[O:2])(=[O:3])(-[C,c,N,n:4])-[OX2:5]-[H]	4	-1.8184615384615386	1.4086213481855594
Sulfinic_acid	[SX3:1](=[O:2])-[O:3]-[H]	2	1.7933333333333332	0.4372070447739835
Phenyl_carboxyl	[c,n,o:1]-[C:2](=[O:3])-[O:4]-[H]	3	3.463441968255319	1.2518054407928614
Carboxyl	[C:1](=[O:2])-[O:3]-[H]	2	3.456652971502591	1.2871420886834017
Thioic_acid	[C,c,N,n:1](=[O,S:2])-[SX2,OX2:3]-[H]	2	0.678267	1.497048763660801
Phenyl_Thiol	[c,n:1]-[SX2:2]-[H]	1	4.978235294117647	2.6137000480499806
# Alkyl_thiol was added on 10/1/2026 and was not determined from the training
# data. Thiol was fit to a set that mixes plain alkanethiols with thiols
# activated by a neighboring carbonyl, amine, or hydroxyl, so its window
# reaches 10.46 and simple thiols such as ethanethiol were deprotonated at
# physiological pH. This entry covers thiols on a saturated carbon that bears
# only carbon or hydrogen, where no atom one bond further is anything but
# carbon, hydrogen, or sulfur, and where the neighboring carbon is neither
# aromatic nor unsaturated. Cysteine (8.3), mercaptoethanol (9.5),
# thioglycolic acid, benzyl mercaptan (9.43), and allyl mercaptan (9.96) are
# all excluded on those grounds and keep the generic rule. The mean and
# standard deviation are computed from the saturated alkanethiols in the pKa
# compilation of R. Williams (ACS Division of Organic Chemistry, updated
# 4/7/2022):
#   ethanethiol 10.50: Danehy JP, Noel CJ (1960) J Am Chem Soc 82:2511
#   1-propanethiol 10.65, 1-butanethiol 10.66, 2-methyl-2-butanethiol 11.21,
#     and 2-methyl-2-propanethiol 11.05: Kreevoy MM et al. (1960) J Am Chem
#     Soc 82:4899
# Two groups reach further than those five compounds support. Sulfur is
# permitted as a neighbor of the alpha and beta carbons, since the thiol's own
# sulfur would otherwise exclude it, so a beta sulfonate also passes:
# 2-mercaptoethanesulfonate (coenzyme M, about 9.1) is claimed. And the
# carbonyl screen stops at the beta carbon, so captopril and its analogues,
# whose carbonyl is three bonds from the sulfur, are claimed as well. Both are
# still protonated below the default ph_max, so the state is right, but the
# mean assigned to them is extrapolated rather than measured.
Alkyl_thiol	[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1;!#16]);!$([CX4]-[#6]~[!#6;!#1;!#16]);!$([CX4]-[#6]=[#6]);!$([CX4]-[#6]#[#6]);!$([CX4]-c):1]-[SX2:2]-[H]	1	10.814	0.3007158126869956

Thiol	[C,N:1]-[SX2:2]-[H]	1	9.12448275862069	1.3317968158171463

# [*]OP(=O)(O[H])O[H]. Note that this matches terminal phosphate of ATP, ADP, AMP.
Phosphate	[PX4:1](=[O:2])(-[OX2:3]-[H])(-[O+0:4])-[OX2:5]-[H]	2	2.4182608695652172	1.1091177991945305	5	6.5055	0.9512787792174668

# Note that Internal_phosphate_polyphos_chain and
# Initial_phosphate_like_in_ATP_ADP were added on 6/2/2020 to better detail with
# molecules that have polyphosphate chains (e.g., ATP, ADP, NADH, etc.). Unlike
# the other protonation states, these two were not determined by analyzing a set
# of many compounds with experimentally determined pKa values.

# For Internal_phosphate_polyphos_chain, we use a mean pKa value of 0.9, per
# DOI: 10.7554/eLife.38821. For the precision value we use 1.0, which is roughly
# the precision of the two ionizable hydroxyls from Phosphate (see above). Note
# that when using recursive SMARTS strings, RDKit considers only the first atom
# to be a match. Subsequent atoms define the environment.
Internal_phosphate_polyphos_chain	[$([PX4:1](=O)([OX2][PX4](=O)([OX2])(O[H]))([OX2][PX4](=O)(O[H])([OX2])))][O:2]-[H]	1	0.9	1.0

# For Initial_phosphate_like_in_ATP_ADP, we use the same values found for the
# lower-pKa hydroxyl of Phosphate (above).
Initial_phosphate_like_in_ATP_ADP	[$([PX4:1]([OX2][C,c,N,n])(=O)([OX2][PX4](=O)([OX2])(O[H])))]O-[H]	1	2.4182608695652172	1.1091177991945305

# [*]P(=O)(O[H])O[H]. Cannot match terminal phosphate of ATP because O not among [C,c,N,n]
Phosphonate	[PX4:1](=[O:2])(-[OX2:3]-[H])(-[C,c,N,n:4])-[OX2:5]-[H]	2	1.8835714285714287	0.5925999820080644	5	7.247254901960784	0.8511476450801531

# Phenol_unactivated was added on 10/1/2026 and was not determined from the
# training data. Phenol was fit to a set spanning nitrophenols (7.1) to plain
# phenol (10.0), so its standard deviation is 3.28 and every phenol, however
# weakly acidic, was deprotonated at physiological pH. This entry covers a
# hydroxyl on an isolated benzene ring whose other five positions carry either
# hydrogen or a saturated carbon bearing nothing but carbon and hydrogen.
# Every activated case therefore keeps the generic rule: nitro, cyano, acyl,
# halo, and trifluoromethyl phenols, catechols and resorcinols, the
# hydroxypyridines, and the naphthols, whose fused-ring carbons fail the
# position test. The mean and standard deviation are computed from phenol and
# its alkyl derivatives in the pKa compilation of R. Williams (ACS Division of
# Organic Chemistry, updated 4/7/2022), which reports phenol 9.95,
# o-, m-, and p-cresol 10.28, 10.08, and 10.19, and o-, m-, and p-ethylphenol
# 10.2, 9.9, and 10.0; the phenol and cresol values are thermodynamic.
Phenol_unactivated	[c:1]1(-[O:2]-[H])[cX3;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1])]):3][cX3;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1])]):4][cX3;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1])]):5][cX3;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1])]):6][cX3;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1])]):7]1	1	10.085714285714285	0.14257829929049987

