# Carboxamide was added on 10/1/2026 and was not determined from the training
# data. Amide was fit to a set that mixes activated amides (pKa as low as 3.4)
# with ordinary ones (13 to 19), so its window reached physiological pH, and
# simple amides, anilides, lactams, and peptide backbone N-H were
# deprotonated. This entry covers only amides whose carbonyl carbon and
# nitrogen bear no substituent other than carbon or hydrogen. Ureas,
# carbamates, and hydrazides still fall through to Amide. The mean is the only
# aqueous N-H pKa of a simple carboxamide in the IUPAC Digitized pKa Dataset
# (doi:10.5281/zenodo.7236452):
#   acetamide 15.1 (25 C): Branch GEK, Clayton JO (1928) J Am Chem Soc
#     50:1680, doi:10.1021/ja01393a023
# A single value has no spread, so the standard deviation of 1.0 follows
# Internal_phosphate_polyphos_chain. At the default precision the window
# starts at pH 14.1.
*Carboxamide	[CX3;!$(C(=O)(-[#7])-[!#6;!#1]):1](=[O:2])-[NX3+0;!$(N(-C=O)-[!#6;!#1]):3]-[H]	2	15.1	1.0

*Amide	[C:1](=[O:2])-[N:3]-[H]	2	12.00611111111111	4.512491341218857
# Primary_sulfonamide was added on 10/1/2026 and was not determined from the
# training data. Sulfonamide was fit to a set that mixes primary sulfonamides
# (around 10) with sulfonylureas and N-heteroaryl sulfonamides (as low as 5),
# so its window reaches 9.90 and unsubstituted sulfonamides such as
# benzenesulfonamide were deprotonated at physiological pH. This entry covers
# only an -S(=O)(=O)NH2 whose sulfur substituent is either a saturated carbon
# bearing nothing but carbon and hydrogen, or a benzene ring whose other
# positions carry hydrogen or such a carbon. The ring test is the one used by
# Phenol_unactivated and excludes the activated aryl sulfonamides, so the
# diuretics and sulfa drugs (hydrochlorothiazide 9.0, furosemide,
# sulfadiazine 6.5, sulfathiazole 7.1) keep the generic rule, as do all
# N-substituted sulfonamides. The mean and standard deviation are computed
# from three compounds, each averaged over its aqueous determinations in
# Reaxys. Only aqueous values were used: the same N-H gives 14 to 25 in
# methanol, ethanol, dimethylsulfoxide, dimethylformamide, and acetonitrile,
# and one reported figure for p-toluenesulfonamide is a hydrogen-bond acidity
# in 1,1,1-trichloroethane rather than an ionization constant at all.
#   benzenesulfonamide 9.80, from 10.0 (25 C, spectrophotometric;
#     Mickeviciute A et al. (2018) Eur Biophys J 47:271-290), 9.41 (25 C,
#     potentiometric; Ludwig M, Pytela O, Kalfus K, Vecera M (1984) Coll
#     Czech Chem Commun 49:1182-1192), and 10.0 (20 C, potentiometric; Willi
#     AV (1956) Helv Chim Acta 39:46)
#   p-toluenesulfonamide 9.89, from 9.61 (25 C, potentiometric; Ludwig et al.
#     1984, as above) and 10.17 (20 C, potentiometric; Willi 1956, as above)
#   methanesulfonamide 10.80, from 10.8 (37 C, potentiometric; Blackburn GM,
#     Tuerkmen H (2005) Org Biomol Chem 3:225-226) and 10.8 (25 C,
#     potentiometric; Hinman RL, Hoogenboom BE (1961) J Org Chem 26:3461-3467)
*Primary_sulfonamide	[SX4:1](=[O:2])(=[O:3])(-[$([CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1;!#16])]),$(c1[cX3;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1])])][cX3;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1])])][cX3;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1])])][cX3;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1])])][cX3;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4;!$([CX4]~[!#6;!#1])])]1):4])-[NX3+0:5]([H:6])[H:7]	4	10.164444444444444	0.552110429104474

*Sulfonamide	[SX4:1](=[O:2])(=[O:3])-[NX3+0:4]-[H]	3	7.9160326086956525	1.9842121316708763

# Adenine_N9_substituted was added on 10/2/2026 and was not determined from
# the training data. Adenine nucleosides and nucleotides (adenosine, AMP, ATP,
# NAD, CoA, SAM) never reached a purine-specific rule. Diazine excludes rings
# with an amino substituent, so N1 and N3 fell to
# Aromatic_nitrogen_unprotonated along with N7 and the substituted N9. That
# window reaches 6.43, so each ring nitrogen came out in both states and ATP
# gave 16 variants at the default pH where it should give 2. At low pH the
# same rule protonated all three ring nitrogens at once, and Anilines_primary
# added a fourth charge on the exocyclic amine, which conjugates into the ring
# and is not a basic site at all.
#
# Only N1 is a site here. N3, N7, N9, and the exocyclic nitrogen are matched
# as context, so they are claimed and no later rule reaches them. N7 and N3
# do protonate on their own (micro pKa 2.15 and 1.5 when N1 is free), but
# those tautomers make up 3.2% and 0.7% of the monocation, and the second
# macro acidity constant of adenosine is -1.53, so above pH 0 adenine carries
# one ring proton at most. Because sites are treated independently, giving
# N3 or N7 a site would bring back the di- and trications.
#
# N9 must carry a substituent. Free adenine has an N9-H acid (or the N7-H
# tautomer), and claiming that nitrogen as context would remove its site.
# C2 and C8 may carry only hydrogen, carbon, or halogen, and the exocyclic
# nitrogen only hydrogen or carbon, with no acyl or sulfonyl group, following
# the Diazine exclusions: donors such as a 2-amino group raise the pKa, and
# N6-acyl adenines are amides with their own sites. Those compounds keep the
# generic rules.
#
# The mean and standard deviation come from the N1 acidity constants of
# 9-substituted adenines (25 C; I = 0.1 M unless noted). Where a value was
# read in a later paper that quotes it, both are given.
#   adenosine 3.64 (I = 0.5 M): Kapinos LE, Operschall BP, Larsen E, Sigel H
#     (2011) Chem Eur J 17:8156-8164, doi:10.1002/chem.201003544. The same
#     paper gives the N7 and N3 micro constants, the -1.53 macro constant,
#     and the tautomer fractions above.
#   9-methyladenine 4.10: Kampf G, Kapinos LE, Griesser R, Lippert B, Sigel H
#     (2002) J Chem Soc Perkin Trans 2, 1320-1327; as quoted in Blindauer
#     CA et al. (2022) New J Chem 46:6484, doi:10.1039/d2nj00543c
#   dAMP 3.97 and dADP 4.00: Mucha A, Knobloch B, Jezowska-Bojczuk M,
#     Kozlowski H, Sigel RKO (2008) Dalton Trans 5368-5377,
#     doi:10.1039/b805911j
#   ADP 3.92: Bianchi EM, Sajadi SAA, Song B, Sigel H (2003) Chem Eur J
#     9:881-892, doi:10.1002/chem.200390109; as quoted in Mucha et al. (2008)
#   ATP 4.00: Tribolet R, Sigel H (1988) Eur J Biochem 170:617-626; as quoted
#     in Sigel A, Sigel H, Sigel RKO (2022) Molecules 27:2625,
#     doi:10.3390/molecules27092625
#   PMEA 4.16: as quoted in Sigel A, Sigel H, Sigel RKO (2022), above
#   HPMPA 4.14 (potentiometric): Song B, Holy A, Sigel H (1994) Gazz Chim
#     Ital 124:387-392; as quoted in Blindauer CA et al. (2022), above
Adenine_N9_substituted	[NX3+0;!$(N-C=[O,S,N]);!$(N-S(=O)=O);!$(N-[!#6;!#1]):1]-[c:2]1:[n+0;X2:3]:[c;!$(c-[!#6;!#1;!F;!Cl;!Br;!I]):4]:[n+0;X2:5]:[c:6]2:[c:7]:1:[n+0;X2:8]:[c;!$(c-[!#6;!#1;!F;!Cl;!Br;!I]):9]:[n+0;X3;H0:10]:2	2	3.99125	0.1653945499188444

# The four entries below were added on 10/2/2026 and were not determined from
# the training data. They do for the other nucleobases what
# Adenine_N9_substituted does for adenine. RDKit treats the oxo-substituted
# rings as aromatic, so none of their nitrogens reached an amide or imide
# entry (those require aliphatic carbon). Each ring nitrogen fell to
# Aromatic_nitrogen_unprotonated (window up to 6.43) and each ring N-H to
# Aromatic_nitrogen_protonated (window 4.22 to 10.12), so at the default pH
# GMP and GTP gave 16 variants and CMP, UMP, and dTMP gave 4, where each
# should give 2. Exocyclic amines also fell to Anilines_primary, and the
# substituted ring nitrogen (N9 or N1) was given an unbuildable site.
#
# Each entry claims the base's other heteroatoms as context so that no later
# rule reaches them. Guanine needs two entries because the N7 site is basic
# and the N1-H site is acidic, and the * prefix applies to a whole entry. Each
# of the two names the other's ring in a recursive constraint rather than
# matching it; ring carbons are never locked, so the shared C4 and C5 can be
# matched by both. The substituted ring nitrogen must carry a carbon, as for
# adenine. Ring carbons may carry only hydrogen or carbon (sp3 carbon for
# uracil), so 5-halo and other activated pyrimidines, whose pKa values differ
# by one to two units, keep the generic rules. The exocyclic amines follow the
# Adenine_N9_substituted exclusions. Fused rings (pteridines, xanthines) are
# excluded by the single-ring tests. Hypoxanthine (inosine) lacks the 2-amino
# group and is not covered.
#
# Values are aqueous, 20 to 25 C, taken from the IUPAC Digitized pKa Dataset
# (doi:10.5281/zenodo.7236452; iupac_high-confidence_v2_4.csv) unless noted.
# Entries the compilers assessed as "Uncertain" were left out. Each compound
# contributes one value, the mean of its listed values, so that well-studied
# nucleosides do not dominate.
#
# Guanine_N9_substituted_N7 (N7-H+ acidity; mean of 3 compounds):
#   guanosine 1.6 (25 C, I = 0.1): Sukhorukov BI, Poltev VI, Blumenfeld LA
#     (1963) Dokl Akad Nauk SSSR 149:1380 [perrin_supp7806]; and 2.11 (25 C,
#     I = 0.1): Sigel H, Massoud SS, Corfu NA (1994) J Am Chem Soc
#     116:2958-2971, as quoted in Song B et al. (1996) Met Based Drugs
#     3:131-141, doi:10.1155/MBD.1996.131
#   dGMP 2.69 (25 C, I = 0.1): Song B et al. (1996), above
#   9-ethylguanine 3.27 (25 C, I = 0.1): Schroder G et al. (1995) J Chem Soc
#     Dalton Trans 3767-3775, as quoted in Song B et al. (1996), above
# *Guanine_N9_substituted_N1H (N1-H acidity; mean of 3 compounds):
#   guanosine 9.21 (25 C): Levene PA, Simms HS (1925) J Biol Chem 65:519
#     [perrin3369]; 9.24 (25 C, I = 0.1): Sukhorukov BI et al. (1963), above
#     [perrin_supp7806]; and 9.25 (25 C): Christensen JJ, Rytting JH, Izatt
#     RM (1970) Biochemistry 9:4907 [serjeant5208]
#   dGMP 9.56 and 9-ethylguanine 9.57 (25 C, I = 0.1): Song B et al. (1996)
# Cytosine_N1_substituted (N3-H+ acidity; mean of 5 compounds):
#   cytidine 4.08 (25 C): Christensen JJ, Rytting JH, Izatt RM (1967) J Phys
#     Chem 71:2700; 4.22 (25 C, I = 0.1): Lewin S, Humphreys DA (1966) J Chem
#     Soc B 210; and 4.22 (25 C, I = 0.1): Sukhorukov BI et al. (1963), above
#     [all perrin_supp7801]
#   cytidine 2'-phosphate 4.32 (25 C, I = 0.2): Anderson DG, Hammes GG, Walz
#     FG (1968) Biochemistry 7:1637 [serjeant4926]
#   cytidine 3'-phosphate 4.21 (25 C, I = 0.1): Bahr JT, Cathou RE, Hammes GG
#     (1965) J Biol Chem 240:3372 [serjeant4927]
#   cytidine 5'-phosphate 4.48 (20 C): Wrobel A, Rabczenko A, Shugar D
#     (1970) Acta Biochim Pol 17:339 [serjeant4928]
#   dCMP 4.46 (25 C, I = 0.1): Song B et al. (1995) Inorg Chim Acta
#     235:99-109, as quoted in Song B et al. (1996), above
# *Uracil_N1_substituted (N3-H acidity; mean of 4 compounds):
#   uridine 9.51 (25 C, I = 0.1): Sukhorukov BI et al. (1963), above
#     [perrin_supp7812]; and 9.25 (25 C): Jonas J, Gut J (1962) Collect Czech
#     Chem Commun 27:716 [serjeant4810]
#   1-methyluracil 9.72 (25 C): Gut J, Prystas M, Jonas J, Sorm F (1961)
#     Collect Czech Chem Commun 26:974; 9.77 (25 C): Nakanishi K, Suzuki N,
#     Yamazaki F (1961) Bull Chem Soc Jpn 34:53; and 9.43 (25 C): Jonas J,
#     Gut J (1962), above [all serjeant2631]
#   thymidine 9.65 (20 C, I = 1.0): Fiskin AM, Beer M (1965) Biochemistry
#     4:1289 [serjeant5218]
#   uridine 5'-phosphate 9.71 (20 C, I = 0.015): Aylward NN (1967) J Chem Soc
#     B 401 [serjeant4914]
Guanine_N9_substituted_N7	[n+0;X2:1]1:[c;!$(c-[!#6;!#1;!F;!Cl;!Br;!I]):2]:[nX3+0;H0:3](-[#6:4]):[c;$(c1:[n+0;X2]:c(-[NX3+0;!$(N-C=[O,S,N]);!$(N-S(=O)=O);!$(N-[!#6;!#1])]):[nX3+0;H1]:c(=[OX1]):c:1):5]:[c:6]:1	0	2.605	0.7113191969854321
*Guanine_N9_substituted_N1H	[nX3+0;H1:1]1:[c:2](-[NX3+0;!$(N-C=[O,S,N]);!$(N-S(=O)=O);!$(N-[!#6;!#1]):3]):[n+0;X2:4]:[c;$(c1:[nX3+0;H0](-[#6]):[c;!$(c-[!#6;!#1;!F;!Cl;!Br;!I])]:[n+0;X2]:c:1):5]:[c:6]:[c:7]:1=[OX1:8]	0	9.454444444444444	0.19155310645508328
Cytosine_N1_substituted	[NX3+0;!$(N-C=[O,S,N]);!$(N-S(=O)=O);!$(N-[!#6;!#1]):1]-[c;R1:2]1:[n+0;X2:3]:[c;R1:4](=[OX1:5]):[nX3+0;H0;R1:6](-[#6:7]):[c;R1;$([cH]),$(c-[#6]):8]:[c;R1;$([cH]),$(c-[#6]):9]:1	2	4.328666666666667	0.14003174243323888
*Uracil_N1_substituted	[nX3+0;H1;R1:1]1:[c;R1:2](=[OX1:3]):[nX3+0;H0;R1:4](-[#6:5]):[c;R1;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4]):6]:[c;R1;$([cH]),$(c-[CX4]):7]:[c;R1:8]:1=[OX1:9]	0	9.595	0.14662878298615262

Anilines_primary	[c:1]-[NX3+0:2]([H:3])[H:4]	1	3.899298673194805	2.068768503987161

# [!#1] tests for a non-hydrogen atom. The earlier [!H] tested for an H count
# other than one, so N-CH(R)R' substituents fell through to the amine group.
# Delocalized nitrogens are excluded for the same reason as in the amine group
# (below).
# Anilines_secondary_N_alkyl was added on 10/1/2026 and was not determined
# from the training data. The Anilines_secondary window reaches 6.51, 0.11
# units past the default ph_min, so every secondary aniline came out in both
# states while the primary and tertiary entries, whose windows stop at 5.97
# and 6.17, correctly gave one. N-methylaniline was therefore doubled while
# aniline and N,N-dimethylaniline, which bracket it in basicity, were not.
# This entry covers anilines whose single N-substituent is a methyl or an
# unbranched alkyl carbon; N-cyclopentyl, N-cyclohexyl, and N-tert-butyl
# anilines are two units more basic (5.30 to 6.95 in the same table) and keep
# the generic rule. Ring substituents are not restricted, because they move
# pKaH further from the default pH window in either direction. The mean and
# standard deviation are computed from the N-alkylaniline column of the
# thermodynamic table of N-substituted anilines in the pKa compilation of
# R. Williams (ACS Division of Organic Chemistry, updated 4/7/2022):
# N-methyl 4.85, N-ethyl 5.11, N-n-propyl 5.02, and N-n-butyl 4.95.
Anilines_secondary_N_alkyl	[c:1]-[NX3+0;!$(N-C=[O,S,N]);!$(N-S(=O)=O);!$(N-N=[O,C]);!$(N-C#N);!$(N-[CX4]-[!#6;!#1]):2]([H:3])[CX4;H2,H3:4]	1	4.9825	0.10996211468804477

Anilines_secondary	[c:1]-[NX3+0;!$(N-C=[O,S,N]);!$(N-S(=O)=O);!$(N-N=[O,C]);!$(N-C#N):2]([H:3])[!#1:4]	1	4.335408163265306	2.1768842022330843
Anilines_tertiary	[c:1]-[NX3+0;!$(N-C=[O,S,N]);!$(N-S(=O)=O);!$(N-N=[O,C]);!$(N-C#N):2]([!#1:3])[!#1:4]	1	4.16690685045614	2.005865735782679

# Diazine was added on 10/1/2026 and was not determined from the training
# data. It covers pyridine-type nitrogens that share a six-membered aromatic
# ring with a second such nitrogen (pyrimidines, pyrazines, pyridazines, and
# their benzo-fused analogs). These are two to five pKa units weaker bases
# than pyridine, but the Aromatic_nitrogen_unprotonated window reaches 6.43.
# Because each ring nitrogen is a separate site, that rule also produced
# dications, whose second pKa is near -6. Rings bearing any exocyclic
# substituent other than carbon, hydrogen, or halogen (e.g., amino, oxo,
# alkoxy) are excluded and keep the generic rule, because donors raise the
# pKa sharply (trimethoprim is ~7). As with Azole_NH, one aqueous pKaH per
# parent heterocycle was taken from the IUPAC Digitized pKa Dataset
# (doi:10.5281/zenodo.7236452):
#   pyrimidine 1.23 and pyridazine 2.24 (20 C): Albert A, Goldacre R,
#     Phillips J (1948) J Chem Soc 2240
#   pyrazine 0.65 (27 C): Chia AS, Trimble RF (1961) J Phys Chem 65:863
#   cinnoline 2.27 (20 C): Osborn AR, Schofield K, Short LN (1956) J Chem
#     Soc 4191
#   phthalazine 3.47 (20 C): Albert A, Armarego WLF, Spinner E (1961) J Chem
#     Soc 2689
#   quinazoline 1.95 (20 C): Bunting JW, Perrin DD (1967) J Chem Soc B 950.
#     This is the pK of the anhydrous cation. The equilibrium value (3.4)
#     includes covalent hydration of the cation, which is not modeled here.
#   quinoxaline 0.56 (20 C): Albert A, Phillips JN (1956) J Chem Soc 1294
Diazine	[n+0;X2;$(n1:[nX2]:a:a:a:a1),$(n1:a:[nX2]:a:a:a1),$(n1:a:a:[nX2]:a:a1);!$(n1:a(!@[!#6;!#1;!F;!Cl;!Br;!I]):a:a:a:a1);!$(n1:a:a(!@[!#6;!#1;!F;!Cl;!Br;!I]):a:a:a1);!$(n1:a:a:a(!@[!#6;!#1;!F;!Cl;!Br;!I]):a:a1):1]	0	1.7671428571428571	1.0330652494028678

# Tetrazole ring nitrogens are excluded. They are roughly nine pKa units less
# basic than the pyridine-type nitrogens this group was fit to, so matching
# them produced tetrazolium variants at physiological pH.
Aromatic_nitrogen_unprotonated	[n+0&H0;!$(n1nnnc1);!$(n1nncn1):1]	0	4.3535441240733945	2.0714072661859584

# The six entries below were added on 10/1/2026 and were not determined from
# the training data. Amines_primary_secondary_tertiary was fit to a set that
# mixes ordinary aliphatic amines (10.2 to 11.3) with amines deactivated by a
# neighboring hydroxyl, ether, nitrile, amide, or second amine (5 to 8), so
# its mean is 8.16 with a standard deviation of 2.52 and its window, 5.64 to
# 10.68, covers the whole default pH range. Every aliphatic amine was
# therefore returned in both states regardless of its substituents.
#
# These entries split that class by the substituents that actually set the
# basicity. Each requires every non-hydrogen neighbor of the nitrogen to be a
# saturated carbon, which also replaces the delocalization exclusions of the
# generic rule below: an amide, sulfonamide, nitrosamine, or cyanamide
# nitrogen has a neighbor that is not sp3 carbon. Each also excludes
# deactivating atoms one bond beyond the alpha carbon, and the alkyl entries
# exclude benzylic, allylic, and propargylic positions, so that the amines
# the generic rule handles correctly keep it: morpholine (8.36), lidocaine,
# the ethylenediamines, the cyanoamines, triallylamine (8.31), and the
# propargylamines (7.05). Aziridines (7.98) are excluded from the secondary
# and tertiary entries by ring size.
#
# The three alkyl entries screen two bonds past the alpha carbon rather than
# one, because remote substituents accumulate: in a 4-substituted piperidine
# the substituent sits at that distance, and the 4-anilidopiperidines run two
# units below piperidine itself (fentanyl 8.44, determined by potentiometric
# titration, sufentanil 8.01, remifentanil 7.1, alfentanil 6.5; the first two
# from Paradis P et al. (2002) Ther Drug Monit 24:768-774, all four as
# compiled in UNODC, Recommended Methods for the Identification and Analysis
# of Fentanyl and its Analogues in Biological Specimens, 2017). All four need
# both states at physiological pH, so that whole class keeps the generic rule.
# The cost is that an amine whose remote heteroatom has little effect also
# falls back to the broad window. The screen reaches three bonds from the
# nitrogen, so a heteroatom further out than that is still ignored:
# 4-aminobutyric acid, whose carboxyl is four bonds away, is claimed, which
# its pKaH of 10.43 makes correct. The benzylic and beta-hydroxy entries
# screen one bond less far, since no comparable series was consulted for them. Unless noted, means and standard
# deviations are computed from the aliphatic amine tables in the pKa
# compilation of R. Williams (ACS Division of Organic Chemistry, updated
# 4/7/2022,
# organicchemistrydata.org/hansreich/resources/pka/pka_data/pka-compilation-williams.pdf),
# whose aliphatic amine values are attributed there to:
#   Hall HK Jr (1957) J Am Chem Soc 79:5441
# and whose fluorinated amine values are attributed there to:
#   Bissell ER, Finger M (1959) J Org Chem 24:1256

# From CF3CH2NH2 5.7, CF3CH2NHCH3 6.05, and CF3CH2N(CH3)2 4.75. The three
# beta fluorines cost roughly 1.7 units each, which is why this entry is
# restricted to a trifluoromethyl on the alpha carbon rather than to
# fluoroalkyl generally; see also Morgenthaler M et al. (2007) ChemMedChem
# 2:1100-1115, doi:10.1002/cmdc.200700059. All three are otherwise plain
# alkylamines, so the mean describes a trifluoromethyl and nothing else.
# Drug-like matches often carry further deactivating groups on the same
# nitrogen, an alpha aryl or a neighboring amide, and those are lower still;
# the state stays neutral either way, but for them this mean is an upper
# bound rather than an estimate.
Fluoroalkylamine	[CX4;$([CX4]-[CX4](F)(F)F):1]-[NX3+0;!$(N-[!$([CX4]);!#1]);!$([N;r3]):2]	1	5.5	0.6726812023536854

# From benzylamine 9.34, N-methylbenzylamine 9.58, N-ethylbenzylamine 9.68,
# N,N-dimethylbenzylamine 8.93, and N,N-diethylbenzylamine 9.48. The aryl
# group costs about one unit, so benzylic amines are treated apart from the
# alkyl entries below rather than with them. All five carry nothing but the
# benzyl group and plain alkyls, so like the alkyl entries this one screens
# heteroatoms out to two bonds past the alpha carbon. The screen was extended
# that far after benchmarking against measured values: N-benzyl pyrrolidines
# bearing a ring trifluoromethyl and ester are 5.40, four units below the
# class mean, and were being returned as cations alone. They now keep the
# generic rule.
#   N-benzyl-3-(trifluoromethyl)pyrrolidine-4-carboxylate, both
#     stereoisomers, 5.40: Novartis test set of Baltruschat M, Czodrowski P
#     (2020) F1000Research 9:113
Benzylic_amine	[CX4;$([CX4]-c):1]-[NX3+0;!$(N-[!$([CX4]);!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]~[!#6;!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]-[#6]~[!#6;!#1]);!$([N;r3]):2]	1	9.402	0.29226700121635363

# From ethanolamine 9.50, N-methylethanolamine 9.87, and propranolol 9.48.
# The latter two are the means of the aqueous determinations in Reaxys:
# N-methylethanolamine 9.82 (potentiometric; Ahmad M, Masohan A, Sawhney
# (2014) Asian J Chem 26:975-980), 9.95 (Xiong D et al. (2013) Green Chem
# 15:1941-1948), and 9.83 (potentiometric; Anelli PL et al. (1997) Gazz Chim
# Ital 127:135-142); propranolol 9.53 (spectrophotometric; Dargo G et al.
# (2020) J Med Chem 63:1763-1774), 9.45 (Gritti F, Guiochon G (2004) Anal
# Chem 76:4779-4789), 9.45 (Siebert GA et al. (2004) J Pharmacol Exp Ther
# 308:228-235), and 9.5 (Lennernas H, Nylander S, Ungell AL (1997) Pharm Res
# 14:667-671). Mixed-solvent determinations were left out, as was a cluster
# of values near -1 that cannot be an amine ionization constant. Amines
# carrying more than one beta hydroxyl are
# excluded, since each costs further basicity (diethanolamine 9.00,
# triethanolamine 7.77) and they are handled acceptably by the generic rule.
# The count is of hydroxyls, not of arms off the nitrogen: tris, whose three
# hydroxymethyls share one alpha carbon, is 8.10 and is excluded on the same
# grounds as diethanolamine.
Beta_hydroxy_amine	[CX4:1]-[NX3+0;$(N-[CX4]-[CX4]-[OX2]-[H]);!$(N(-[CX4]-[CX4]-[OX2])-[CX4]-[CX4]-[OX2]);!$(N-[CX4](-[CX4]-[OX2])-[CX4]-[OX2]);!$(N-[!$([CX4]);!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]~[!#6;!#1;!$([OX2]-[H])]);!$([N;r3]):2]	1	9.61638888888889	0.21692345893664236

# From methylamine 10.62, ethylamine 10.63, n-propylamine 10.53,
# n-butylamine 10.59, sec-butylamine 10.56, tert-butylamine 10.55,
# isopropylamine 10.63, cyclohexylamine 10.64, cyclohexylmethylamine 10.49,
# and neopentylamine 10.21.
Alkylamine_primary	[CX4:1]-[NX3+0;H2;!$(N-[!$([CX4]);!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]~[!#6;!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]-[#6]~[!#6;!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]=[#6]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]#[#6]);!$(N-[CX4]-c):2]	1	10.545	0.12773670837573134

# From dimethylamine 10.64, diethylamine 10.98, di-n-propylamine 11.00,
# diisopropylamine 11.05, di-n-butylamine 11.25, diisobutylamine 10.50,
# pyrrolidine 11.27, piperidine 11.22, and azetidine 11.29.
Alkylamine_secondary	[CX4:1]-[NX3+0;H1;!$(N-[!$([CX4]);!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]~[!#6;!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]-[#6]~[!#6;!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]=[#6]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]#[#6]);!$(N-[CX4]-c);!$([N;r3]):2]	1	11.022222222222222	0.28415566938641984

# From trimethylamine 9.76, triethylamine 10.65, tri-n-propylamine 10.65,
# tri-n-butylamine 10.89, dimethylethylamine 9.99, dimethyl-n-propylamine
# 9.99, dimethyl-n-butylamine 10.02, dimethylisopropylamine 10.30,
# dimethylisobutylamine 9.91, dimethyl-sec-butylamine 10.40, and
# dimethyl-tert-butylamine 10.52.
Alkylamine_tertiary	[CX4:1]-[NX3+0;H0;!$(N-[!$([CX4]);!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]~[!#6;!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]-[#6]~[!#6;!#1]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]=[#6]);!$(N-[CX4]-[#6]#[#6]);!$(N-[CX4]-c);!$([N;r3]):2]	1	10.28	0.3689715436182039

# Nitrogens whose lone pair is delocalized into an adjacent C=O, C=S, C=N,
# S(=O)=O, N=O, N=C, or C#N are excluded. They are not basic in water, and
# matching them protonated, e.g., tertiary amides, thioureas, and nitrosamines.
Amines_primary_secondary_tertiary	[C:1]-[NX3+0;!$(N-C=[O,S,N]);!$(N-S(=O)=O);!$(N-N=[O,C]);!$(N-C#N):2]	1	8.159107682388349	2.5183597445318147

# e.g., [*]P(=O)(O[H])[*]. Note that cannot match the internal phosphates of ATP, because
# oxygen is not among [C,c,N,n,F,Cl,Br,I]
Phosphinic_acid	[PX4:1](=[O:2])(-[C,c,N,n,F,Cl,Br,I:3])(-[C,c,N,n,F,Cl,Br,I:4])-[OX2:5]-[H]	4	2.9745	0.6867886750744557

# e.g., [*]OP(=O)(O[H])O[*]. Cannot match ATP because P not among [C,c,N,n,F,Cl,Br,I]
Phosphate_diester	[PX4:1](=[O:2])(-[OX2:3]-[C,c,N,n,F,Cl,Br,I:4])(-[O+0:5]-[C,c,N,n,F,Cl,Br,I:4])-[OX2:6]-[H]	6	2.7280434782608696	2.5437448856908316

# e.g., [*]P(=O)(O[H])O[*]. Cannot match ATP because O not among [C,c,N,n,F,Cl,Br,I].
Phosphonate_ester	[PX4:1](=[O:2])(-[OX2:3]-[C,c,N,n,F,Cl,Br,I:4])(-[C,c,N,n,F,Cl,Br,I:5])-[OX2:6]-[H]	5	2.0868	0.4503028610465036

Primary_hydroxyl_amine	[C,c:1]-[O:2]-[NH2:3]	2	4.035714285714286	0.8463816543155368
*Indole_pyrrole	[c;R:1]1[c;R:2][c;R:3][c;R:4][n;R:5]1[H]	4	14.52875	4.06702491591416

# Azole_NH was added on 10/1/2026. Like Internal_phosphate_polyphos_chain and
# Initial_phosphate_like_in_ATP_ADP, it was not determined from the training
# data. Without it, the N-H of imidazoles, pyrazoles, triazoles, benzimidazoles,
# and purines falls through to Aromatic_nitrogen_protonated (pKa 7.17), so these
# rings were deprotonated at physiological pH. The mean and standard deviation
# are computed from one experimental aqueous N-H pKa per parent heterocycle, so
# the standard deviation reflects the spread between ring types rather than
# experimental scatter within a class. Sources were traced through the IUPAC
# Digitized pKa Dataset (doi:10.5281/zenodo.7236452):
#   imidazole 14.52 (25 C): Walba H, Isensee RW (1956) J Org Chem 21:702
#   benzimidazole 12.78 (25 C): Walba H, Isensee RW (1961) J Org Chem
#     26:2789-2791, doi:10.1021/jo01066a039
#   pyrazole 14.21 (25 C): Yagil G (1967) Tetrahedron 23:2855-2861,
#     doi:10.1016/0040-4020(67)85151-2 (aqueous KOH, H_ acidity scale)
#   1,2,4-triazole 10.26 (20 C): Kroger CF, Freiberg W (1965) Z Chem 5:381
#   1,2,3-triazole 9.4: Albert A (1963) Physical Methods in Heterocyclic
#     Chemistry, Vol. 1, p. 98 (compiled value; primary source not yet verified)
# Tetrazoles (N-H pKa ~4.9) are deliberately excluded and still match
# Aromatic_nitrogen_protonated. Rings bearing an exocyclic C=O or C=S (e.g.,
# 1,2,4-oxadiazol-5-ones) are also excluded, because they are far more acidic.
*Azole_NH	[n;$(n1naaa1),$(n1anaa1);!$(n1nnnc1);!$(n1nncn1);!$(n1c(=[O,S])aaa1);!$(n1ac(=[O,S])aa1):1]-[H]	0	12.234	2.3106665704943237
*Aromatic_nitrogen_protonated	[n:1]-[H]	0	7.17	2.94602395490212
